      "text": "Symptoms I reported (team-written checklist, not a validated instrument):
- I snore loudly: sometimes
- I wake up choking or gasping: not answered
- Someone has noticed pauses in my breathing during sleep: not answered
- I wake up feeling unrefreshed: often
- I feel very sleepy during the day: often
- I wake up with headaches: not answered
- Have you fallen asleep, or nearly fallen asleep, while driving? no",
      "title": "What I reported",
    },
    {
      "id": "screening",
      "source": "user-reported",
      "text": "NoSAS questionnaire score: 5 of 17. Below the published cutoff of 8 (this cannot rule out sleep apnea).
Your NoSAS score is 5 of 17. In the studies that tested it, people scoring 8 or more were more likely to have clinically significant sleep-disordered breathing than people scoring lower. A lower score cannot rule out sleep apnea. Talk to a clinician if you have symptoms. NoSAS (Marti-Soler et al., Lancet Respir Med 2016, doi:10.1016/S2213-2600(16)30075-3) is a screening score. It is not a diagnosis. This score is being applied outside the populations in which it was validated.
Source: Marti-Soler H, et al. The NoSAS score for screening of sleep-disordered breathing: a derivation and validation study. Lancet Respir Med 2016;4:742-748. doi:10.1016/S2213-2600(16)30075-3.",
      "title": "Questionnaire score (NoSAS)",
    },
    {
      "id": "audio",
      "source": "missing",
      "text": "Not provided. No recording was analyzed.
A quiet, normal-looking, or inconclusive recording cannot rule out sleep apnea.",
      "title": "Recording observations",
    },
    {
      "id": "guidance",
      "source": "app-guidance",
      "text": "Based on what you shared, this is worth discussing with a clinician.
Your symptoms and questionnaire answers suggest this is worth discussing, and a quiet or missing recording cannot rule out sleep apnea.
Missing information:
- Audio recording (optional)
Uncertainty:
- SleepSmooth is informational and is not a diagnosis. It cannot confirm or rule out sleep apnea.
- A quiet, normal-looking, or inconclusive recording cannot rule out sleep apnea.
- Your answers and the recording are shown separately. They are not combined into one score.",
      "title": "What this app can and cannot say",
    },
    {
      "id": "questions",
      "source": "app-guidance",
      "text": "- Given my symptoms, is an evaluation for sleep apnea appropriate for me?
- What kind of sleep testing, if any, would you suggest?
- Could anything else be causing my tiredness or snoring?
- What should I watch for before my next visit?",
      "title": "Questions for my clinician",
    },
    {
      "id": "limits",
      "source": "app-guidance",
      "text": "SleepSmooth is informational and is not a diagnosis. It cannot confirm or rule out sleep apnea.
Audio analysis detects acoustic patterns only. It does not directly measure airflow or confirm apnea events.
A quiet, normal-looking, or inconclusive recording cannot rule out sleep apnea.",
      "title": "Limitations",
    },
  ],
  "userEdited": false,
}
`;

